NEARS-2494 QA — side finding, NOT one of AC1-3 (distinct window from the flagged
_syncItem ~1118-1133 race). Reproduced with an intentionally long (~79s) backend
stall via SIGSTOP, exceeding ApiClient's 40s client-side timeout.

Sequence (dedicated QA backend, port 8040, DB multi_food_db_qa2494, user_id=6, item_id=27):
1. Backend SIGSTOPped. Tap Add To Cart (gen1) -> ADD dispatches, blocked at server.
2. Decrement to 0 before response (item unpersisted) -> tombstones gen1, local row removed.
3. Immediately re-add (gen2) -> local row recreated, second ADD debounced-but-queued
   behind gen1 in _inFlightAdd (reschedule branch — never dispatches while gen1 is
   still outstanding).
4. ~40s later, the app's OWN ApiClient client-side timeout fires for gen1 (server
   never responded because still SIGSTOPped) -> gen1 hits the onlineCartList==null
   branch in _syncItem, which unconditionally calls
   _clearAbandonedIfMatches(itemId, myGeneration) even though the request may still
   be sitting in the OS socket queue and land server-side later. No cleanup DELETE
   is ever scheduled for gen1 in this branch (only the success/non-current-generation
   branch schedules one).
5. Once _inFlightAdd clears (gen1's finally), gen2's rescheduled debounce fires and
   dispatches for real -> also blocked (backend still SIGSTOPped).
6. SIGCONT at ~79s total stall. Both requests, having been sitting in the kernel
   accept queue the whole time, are then processed by the PHP dev server in order:
     - gen1's "abandoned" ADD lands anyway -> Admin/CentralLogics cart-add logic
       finds no existing row for (user 6, item 27) yet -> creates cart row, qty=1.
     - gen2's ADD lands immediately after -> finds the row gen1 JUST created for the
       same (user, item, module) -> increments it instead of creating a duplicate ->
       final server-side quantity = 2.

Observed flutter run log (this repro, ~16:05:15-16:06:35):
  [NET] POST endpoint=/api/v1/customer/cart/add                     (gen1 dispatch)
  [FAIL] endpoint=/api/v1/customer/cart/add http_status=null type=ApiFailure
         transport_error=timeout msg="api request threw"
         correlation_id=3c742a68-bfad-4e53-9944-02e14c38c4b3        (gen1 client timeout, ~40s)
  [NET] POST endpoint=/api/v1/customer/cart/add                     (gen2 dispatch, rescheduled)
  [NET] endpoint=/api/v1/customer/cart/add http_status=200          (gen2 success)

DB before (during stall, no row yet for item 27):
  (empty)
DB after CONT (single row, unexpectedly qty=2, not the re-added qty=1):
  id=529 user_id=6 item_id=27 quantity=2 created_at=2026-08-25 16:06:34 updated_at=2026-08-25 16:06:35

App-side (local, authoritative UI) quantity for item 27 at this point: 1
  (ui_list dump captured "1" next to Salmon Nigiri (5pc) card, "Remove" stepper shown)

Effect: local cart shows qty=1 (what the user actually intended after the re-add),
server silently holds qty=2 (the orphaned gen1 add's row, incremented by gen2's own
add) — a silent quantity divergence/over-charge risk with no error surfaced to the
user and no log flagging the divergence. Confirmed by removing the row via the app's
own Remove action afterward (real DELETE, not raw DML) and re-querying — 0 rows,
consistent with a single row having existed.

Root cause (hypothesis, not confirmed against source beyond what's visible here):
_syncItem's onlineCartList==null branch (fix-cycle-1 F1 failure-branch) clears the
generation's tombstone unconditionally on ANY add failure, including a CLIENT-SIDE
timeout where the request's actual server-side fate is unknown — there is no
"maybe-landed" cleanup scheduled for that case, unlike the success-but-stale branch
(~1118-1133) which does schedule one. A sufficiently slow/degraded real network
(>40s round trip on the ADD call) could reach this window in production without any
artificial stall.

Scope note: this required an abnormally long real-world stall (79s, exceeding the
40s client timeout) to hit deterministically — NOT the ~3-5s widened window the
ticket's repro technique describes for AC1/AC2, and distinct from the specific
non-current-generation SUCCESS-branch race the ticket self-flagged
(~cart_controller.dart:1118-1133). Filed as a regression_bugs candidate, not a
task_bug against AC1-3, and does NOT block this ticket's PASS.
